# The moment (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, phase 7), region by region: each country's weather as only it has it, its own
# festivals, its places and its tales (Docs/Design/60: Ridgemere on its tiers above the Sound; Frosthollow under the
# Whitefangs; Coldwater on Glasswater; Oakhaven in the Warmwood; Mirebridge in the Sallow Fens; Larkfield on the
# downs; Southmeadow in the Sunfields and the Orchard Vale). Extends the region files; doesn't repeat them.
# Drafts, to tune by ear.

# ================================================================ Ridgemere: the rain city

scene m7_rg_rain_tiers
when topic=weather here.region=ridgemere here.weather=rain
a : Rain runs downhill. Heights to the Sump, every drop. | The stairs are waterfalls again. | You can tell the quarter by what's in the gutter. | Every retaining wall's weeping.
b[quarter=sump] #sump : And we get all of it. Theirs and ours. | Down in the Sump we wade. Every lane. | I've a river through my kitchen. Free of charge.
b[quarter=heights] #heights : Our drains are excellent. I couldn't say about further down.
b : Gravity's got no manners. | Hold the rail. | Flowers up top, worse down here. | Walls cry. City's sad.
a@sump : Theirs and ours, and the fish. | Gods. | Charge them for it.
a@heights : I'm sure you couldn't.
a : None at all. | What rail? | Best not look. | City's wet. Same thing.

scene m7_rg_rain_names
when topic=weather here.region=ridgemere here.weather=rain
a : Is it mizzle or drizzle? | My grandmother had nine words for rain. | Spitting, they call this. Spitting! | That's soft rain. Wets you to the bone and pretends it didn't.
b[from=ridgemere] #city : City-born know: mizzle's finer. Drizzle's got intent.
b : Wet. It's wet. | Only nine? | It's spitting on me. | Liar rain.
a@city : Intent. I like that.
a : You've no poetry. | She'd need a tenth today. | Rude rain. | The worst kind.

scene m7_rg_sun_out
when topic=weather here.region=ridgemere here.weather=clear
a : Sun's out! Everyone stop! | Look at them all, faces up like flowers. | The Sound's blue. Actually blue. | I'd forgotten what the Rainward Hills look like.
b[from=ridgemere] #city : Don't get used to it. Never get used to it.
b : Quick, hang out everything you own. | Like cats on a sill. | It's lying. It's grey underneath. | Green. Same as always.
a@city : Too late. I'm used to it.
a : Already did. | Purring, the lot of them. | Hush. | Greener than I remembered.

scene m7_rg_fog_sound
when topic=weather here.region=ridgemere here.weather=fog here.place=shore|street|work
a : Fog's come in off the Sound. | Can't see the water gates. | The fog horn's lowing out on the point like a lost cow. | Masts sticking out of the #fog# like reeds.
b[job=sailor|fisher] #sea : Nobody's leaving harbour. Nobody sane. | I've rowed in this. Never again. Heard the rocks before I saw them. | Fog's the sea's way of saying stay home.
b : It'll sit till the tide turns. | Can hear them, though. Creaking. | Somebody find it its mother. | Ships in a cloud.
a@sea : Somebody insane, then. There's always one. | Gods. | Then I'll listen to the sea.
a : Or all week. | Creak, creak. | Ha. Poor beast. | Ghost ships.

scene m7_rg_wharf_dawn
when topic=day|weather here.region=ridgemere here.place=shore|work here.time=dawn
a : Boats coming in. | Gulls already at the Fish Hall. | Tar and herring and wet rope, all at once. | Somebody's singing on the Ropewalk.
b[job=fisher|sailor] #sea : Good catch tonight, by the look of the boats. | Bad night out there. Half the boats are riding high. | Greatly boats first in, as ever.
b : Breakfast arriving. | Before the buyers. | Smell of the wharf. | Always someone.
a@sea : I'll have a herring off one of them. | Poor souls. | Of course they are.
a : Cheaper at the Sump Market. | Gulls are early birds. | And home. | Rope song.

scene m7_rg_storm_sound
when topic=weather here.region=ridgemere here.weather=storm
a : Waves over the wharf wall. | They've closed the water gates. | The Greatly Yard's lashing everything down. | Somebody's boat's slipped her mooring.
b[quarter=wharf|docks] #wharf : My door's under it. Again.
b : Sound's angry. | Good. Keeps the sea out. | Hull'll be fine. Greatly builds well. | Hope she comes back.
a@wharf : Come uphill to ours. Bring whoever's yours.
a : Very. | And us in. | They'd better. | Boats know the way.

scene m7_rg_heights_view
when topic=weather|day here.region=ridgemere a.quarter=heights here.weather=clear|overcast
a : From the garden you can see clear to the Rainwood. | The whole Sound's laid out like a map. | You can see the smoke from the works. Dreadful. | Look how small the wharf looks from up here.
b[quarter=sump|works|wharf] #below : Looks different from underneath. | We can see you too. From down there. | Come down and see it close. Bring a cloth for your nose.
b : Lovely view. | Like a painting. | Can't see it from mine. | Small and smelly.
a@below : ...I didn't mean it like that. | ...Quite. | I'm sorry. Truly.
a : Isn't it. | Better. It moves. | Lucky you. | Quaint.

scene m7_rg_sump_wet
when topic=weather|day here.region=ridgemere here.weather=rain|storm a.quarter=sump
a : The lane's a river. | Water's in the tenement cellar again. | The Bilge has a puddle you could drown in. | Rats are moving upstairs.
b : Every time. | Mind the rats. | Somebody did, last year. | Smart rats.
a : And every time they promise to mend the drain. | They mind me. | Gods. | Smarter than the landlord.

scene m7_rg_commons_market
when topic=day here.region=ridgemere here.day=market here.place=market a.quarter=commons|harbour_hill|office
a : The Commons Market's heaving. | Pies at the Commons Market, the good ones. | Cheese wolf's shouting again. | Somebody from the Heights slumming it.
b : Mind your purse. | Go before they're gone. | Loudest cheese in the city. | Buying sausage like it's scandal.
a : Always. | Gone already. | Best cheese too. | It is, for them.

scene m7_rg_sump_market
when topic=day here.region=ridgemere here.place=market a.quarter=sump
a : Soup's thin today at the Sump Market. | Nuts for a penny. Rotten for free. | Somebody pinched a sausage and the whole market cheered. | Pickles again. Always pickles.
b : Thin as the purse. | Free's my price. | Good for them. | Pickles keep.
a : Thinner. | Mine too. | Sausage hero. | So does misery.

scene m7_rg_office_square
when topic=day here.region=ridgemere here.place=street|hall
a : Office Square's full of petitioners again. | Somebody's tied a ribbon on the statue. | The fountain's running green. Who did that? | Clerks out for their bread at midday, all at once. Like starlings.
b[job=official] #clerk : Never a dull day on Office Square. I'd know. I'm stuck in it.
b : Rents due. | It'll be off by noon. | The Blue Vats lads, I'd bet. | And just as noisy.
a@clerk : Poor you.
a : Always rents. | Shame. | Bet you're right. | Noisier.

scene m7_rg_restday_tide
when topic=day here.region=ridgemere here.day=rest
a : The Chapel of the Tide was full this morning. | Restday bells from every quarter. | The works are quiet. You can hear the gulls. | Even the Houses keep Restday. Mostly.
b[quarter=heights] #heights : One hears the bells best from the Heights, of course.
b : Tide waits for no one, but it waits for that. | Bells from every quarter, and no two together. | Strange, without the hammering. | Mostly.
a@heights : Of course one does.
a : Sermon was long. | Like a quarrel in brass. | Peaceful. | Greatly's ships still sail.

scene m7_rg_fest_wharf
when topic=festival here.region=ridgemere topic.when=today here.place=shore|street
a : Every boat in the harbour's hung with lanterns. | They're racing skiffs across the Sound at noon. | The Fish Hall's giving out herring. Free. | House colours on every mast.
b[job=sailor|fisher] #sea : There's skiff racing later. My cousin's rowing for the Greatly Yard.
b : Pretty on the water. | Who's favourite? | Free herring, best festival. | Greatly blue wins on numbers.
a@sea : A penny on your cousin, then.
a : Prettier at night. | The Ropewalk lads. | I'll take two. | Always does.

scene m7_rg_fest_soon_houses
when topic=festival here.region=ridgemere topic.when=soon
a : The Houses are each swearing to outdo the others for #here.festival#. | Mossgrave's promised woollen favours for every child. | Greatly wants a lantern on every boat in the harbour. | The Council's voting on the feast. They'll vote for themselves.
b : And we'll pay for it, somehow. | Itchy favours. | Every boat? Even the leaky ones? | Always do.
a : Always us. | Warm, though. | Especially those. They'll sink prettily. | Like cats at a bowl.

scene m7_rg_from_away_city
when topic=weather|day here.region=ridgemere a.from=frosthollow|coldwater|oakhaven|mirebridge|larkfield|southmeadow pair.same_from=yes
a : Another day in the city. Remember the sky at home? | Do you ever get used to all these stairs? | Too many wolves. Can't hear yourself. | Somebody's cooking like my mother did. I followed my nose two streets.
b : Every day. | Never. | I miss the quiet too. | It gets you like that. Out of nowhere.
a : We'll go back. One day. | My knees won't. | Quiet's a luxury here. | Every time.
b?[warmth=warm] : Till then, we've each other. And the rain.

scene m7_rg_history_wall
when topic=lore here.region=ridgemere
a : They say the city wall was built in a single summer. | The first Ridgemere was just the wharf and a chapel. | The Heights were a sheep run once. | Office Square was the old fish market.
b[stage=old] #old : My grandfather carried stone for the East Gate. He said the city was half the size then.
b : A summer and a lot of beatings. | And look at it now. | Still full of sheep, some say. | Still smells like one.
a@old : Proud of it, was he?
a : Grim. | Wet and proud. | Ha! | It does, after rain.

scene m7_rg_lore_drowned_lantern
when topic=lore here.region=ridgemere here.time=dusk|night
a : Why's it called the Drowned Lantern? | Somebody saw a light under the water at the end of the pier. | They say the Sound's got a town under it. Bells and all. | Fishers say a lamp on the water at night is a drowned wolf looking for home.
b[quarter=sump|wharf] #local : Ask anyone in the Sump about lights under the water. The Drowned Lantern's named for one.
b : Nobody remembers. | Lantern fish. | And fish for mayors. | Then don't row to it.
a@local : Gods. Is that true?
a : Somebody does. | Or a ghost. | Ha. | Nobody does, twice.
b?@local : True as the rain.

scene m7_rg_lore_houses_feud
when topic=lore here.region=ridgemere
a : Why do Fell and Grayrock hate each other? | They say a Greatly once married a fishwife, and the city's never got over it. | Mossgrave was a shepherd's name, once. Now look. | Every House started with one stubborn wolf.
b[b.house] #house : We don't speak of it. House business.
b : A tree fell on a quarry. Or a quarry fell on a tree. | Best thing Greatly ever did. | Wool's money. | And ended with a lot of them.
a@house : Of course not.
a : Depends who you ask. | Shame they don't do it more. | Sheep make lords. | Ha.

# ================================================================ Frosthollow: the high valley

scene m7_fh_snow_dawn_market
when topic=weather|day here.region=frosthollow here.weather=snow here.time=dawn here.day=market
a : Marketday, and the snow's on the stalls before the goods. | Who's going to climb up for market in this? | Traders'll be stuck halfway up the Pass Road. | Sweep your stall or sell snow.
b[role=merchant] #trade : I'll sell snow if they'll pay. Iron-cold snow, best in the realm. | I've brought a brazier. A penny a warm. | Snow keeps the cheese. I'm not complaining.
b : Only us fools. | Those that must. | Then we buy from each other. | Snow's free.
a@trade : I'll give you a penny for a sackful. | Here's two pennies. My paws are dead. | You'd sell your own tail if it froze.
a : Fools' market. | Must's a hard master. | Same old prices. | Free's too dear.

scene m7_fh_lamp_dark
when topic=weather|day here.region=frosthollow here.season=winter here.time=dusk|night
a : Dark by mid-afternoon. Lamps all day. | The valley's in shadow till noon, now. | Lamplight on the snow. Blue and gold. | Peaks catch the last light. Then nothing.
b[job=miner] #miner : Dark's no change for me. Dark's my trade. | Down the seam it's always midnight. Up here, it's a holiday. | I see the sun on Restdays. If it shows.
b : Oil's dearer than bread. | Sun forgets us up here. | Prettiest thing in the world. | And then cold.
a@miner : You've got used to it? | Some holiday. | It won't.
a : Everything's dearer. | It remembers in spring. | It is. | Colder.

scene m7_fh_avalanche
when topic=weather|lore here.region=frosthollow here.season=winter|spring
a : Heard the slide on the east face last night. | Snow's hanging over the pass like a breath held. | Pass Road's shut till the slide's cleared. | The thaw's loosening the high snow.
b[stage=old] #old : The great slide took half the old houses when I was a pup. Same face.
b : Anyone under it? | It'll let go soon. | Again? Third time this winter. | Keep off the slopes.
a@old : Gods. And they built again?
a : Nobody, thank the lamp. | Then the pass shuts. | Fourth. | Keep off everything.
b?@old : We always build again. That's Frosthollow.

scene m7_fh_clear_peaks
when topic=weather here.region=frosthollow here.weather=clear
a : Whitefangs are sharp today. Like you could cut yourself. | You can see the Greyteeth from here, clear. | Snow glare's blinding. Squint and walk. | Sky's so blue it hurts.
b : Days like this you remember why. | Teeth and fangs. Lovely names for mountains. | Wear a cloth over your eyes. | Hurts and heals.
a : Remember why what? | Mountains bite. | I'll wear my scarf. | Lamp-sure.

scene m7_fh_summer_short
when topic=weather|day here.region=frosthollow here.season=summer
a : Two weeks of summer. Don't blink. | Bilberries up on the slope! Pick them while they last. | Snow's still in the gullies, and it's high summer. | Pups are swimming in the meltwater. Mad things.
b[from=oakhaven|mirebridge|larkfield|southmeadow] #south : You call this summer? I'm still in my coat.
b : Blinked. Missed it. | Pick fast. Summer's short. | Never leaves, that snow. | Mad and blue.
a@south : Best two weeks in the realm.
a : Ha. | Paws blue already. | Old friend, that snow. | Blue and happy.

scene m7_fh_smelter
when topic=day|weather here.region=frosthollow here.place=work|street
a : Smelter's smoke's lying low today. | Ore carts screaming down the track. | You can taste the iron in the air. | The smelter's warm. Everyone's loitering.
b[job=miner|smith] #iron : That's money, that is. Smoke, carts, the lot.
b : Bad air. | Grease them, somebody. | Taste of home. | Me included.
a@iron : Smells like rust.
a : Hold your breath. | Nobody will. | Rust and home. | Warmest loafers in the valley.

scene m7_fh_midwinter
when topic=festival here.region=frosthollow here.season=winter
a : The Snow Chapel's hung with miners' lamps for #here.festival#. | Blood sausage and hot ale, the whole valley. | They'll read out the names of the lost at the Snow Chapel. | The smelter's banked down for #here.festival#. First quiet in a season.
b[job=miner] #miner : I'll carry my father's lamp. Always do.
b : Prettiest sight in the world. | Sausage first. | Every one, and slow. | You can hear the stream again.
a@miner : He'd be proud.
a : From the road it looks like the stars came down. | Ale first. | Every one remembered. | Strange, the quiet.

scene m7_fh_lore_ice_wolf
when topic=lore here.region=frosthollow
a : My grandmother swore she saw a white wolf on the high snow. Big as a horse. | They say the Whitefangs were real fangs once. A giant's. | Never sleep in the snow. It sings you down. | The old trappers left meat on the high rocks. For something.
b[job=miner] #miner : Miners know. There's things up high no wolf's named.
b : Hungry, was it? | Giant with bad teeth. | I'll stay awake, then. | For what?
a@miner : Don't. I've to walk home.
a : She didn't stay to ask. | And bad breath, I'd say. | Wise. | Nobody'd say.

scene m7_fh_southerner_snow
when topic=weather here.region=frosthollow here.weather=snow b.from=oakhaven|mirebridge|larkfield|southmeadow
a : First winter up here? | You'll want better boots. | Snow getting to you? | You look froze to the marrow.
b : Is it always like this? Always? | Better everything. | It's in me. In my bones. | I'm a southern wolf. We melt or we freeze. No middle.
a : Gets worse. | Go see the cobbler. | That's Frosthollow saying hello. | You'll learn the middle.
b : Gods help me. | Which way? Quick. | Tell it to say goodbye. | I'll die learning it.

# ================================================================ Coldwater: on Glasswater

scene m7_cw_lake_frozen
when topic=weather here.region=coldwater here.season=winter
a : The lake's frozen out to the far reeds. | Ice fishers out on Glasswater already. | Hear the ice? It sings at night. | Somebody's cut a hole and lost a hat down it.
b[job=fisher] #fisher : Ice is thick enough at the edge. Don't trust the middle. Never the middle.
b : Thin at the river mouth, mind. | Mad. | Like whales. Or ghosts. | The lake keeps hats.
a@fisher : Never the middle.
a : Always is. | Hungry, more like. | Ghost whales. | And boots. And wolves.

scene m7_cw_mist_morning
when topic=weather here.region=coldwater here.time=dawn here.weather=fog|clear|overcast
a : The lake's smoking this morning. | The lake's breathing this morning. | The far shore's gone. | Not a ripple on the water.
b[job=fisher] #fisher : Still water, good catch. Get out there. | Mist on the lake means the fish come up. | I've had my nets out since dark.
b : Pretty, that. | Cold breath. | It's there. Probably. | Throw a stone. Spoil it.
a@fisher : After breakfast. | Go on, then. | Anything in them?
a : For an hour. | Lake breath. | Probably. | You would.

scene m7_cw_storm_lake
when topic=weather here.region=coldwater here.weather=storm|rain
a : Waves on Glasswater. You don't see that often. | Boats are dragged up to the willows. | The lake's gone grey and ugly. | The High Road'll be a stream.
b : Something's stirring it. | Good. Nobody out. | Grey lake, grey mood. | Nobody coming up today.
a : Wind. Just wind. | Except Old Heron. | Mine too. | Or down.

scene m7_cw_summer_swim
when topic=weather|day here.region=coldwater here.season=summer here.weather=clear
a : Pups in the shallows all day. | Hot enough to swim. Nearly. | Warm on top, and ice a paw below. | Dragonflies all over the reeds.
b[parent=yes] #parent : Mine are in there somewhere. Stay where you can stand, I've told them.
b : Keep them shallow. | Nearly's not enough. | That's the joy of it. | Blue ones. Pretty.
a@parent : Told them. Ha. They listen like fish.
a : Always. | I'll dip a paw. | Some joy. | And biting ones.

scene m7_cw_long_river
when topic=lore here.region=coldwater
a : Glasswater gives the Long River its start. Every drop to the fens. | Throw a reed in here and it reaches the Drowned Delta in a month. | They say the river remembers the lake. | Everything in the south drinks our water.
b[from=mirebridge] #fen : Arrives muddier, I'll tell you that.
b : And never thanks us. | Send a letter that way. | Rivers are sentimental. | Fair trade for their apples.
a@fen : Fens'll do that to anything.
a : Never. | Cheaper than a carter. | So am I. | Fair enough.

scene m7_cw_fest_lake
when topic=festival here.region=coldwater
a : Ribbons on every boat for #here.festival#. | They'll float candles on the lake at dusk. | Somebody's to swim for the luck again. | The first catch goes to the chapel, festival or no.
b : Boats look happy. | Prettiest thing on the water. | Not me. Not in that cold. | As it should.
a : Boats are always happy. | Every candle a wish. | Coward. | Always.

# ================================================================ Oakhaven: the Warmwood

scene m7_oh_canopy_rain
when topic=weather here.region=oakhaven here.weather=rain
a : Rain's on the leaves before it's on us. | Under the oaks you hardly feel it. | Hear it? Like a thousand pups tapping. | Drip from the trees long after it stops.
b[from=ridgemere] #city : In the city you'd be drowned by now.
b : Best roof in the realm. | Till you step out from under. | Sleepy sound. | Second rain, my granddam called it.
a@city : The Warmwood keeps us dry.
a : Older than any House. | Then soaked. | Makes me yawn. | Good name.

scene m7_oh_autumn_wood
when topic=weather|day here.region=oakhaven here.season=autumn
a : Pigs in the wood up to their ears in acorns. | The whole wood's gone copper. | Mushrooms up after the rain. | Leaf mould to the ankle.
b[job=woodcutter|crafter] #wood : Good felling weather. Sap's down.
b : Fat pigs, fat winter. | Prettiest month. | Pick the right ones. | Smells like home.
a@wood : Leave us a tree or two.
a : Fat everything. | Coppery. | Which are right? | It is home.

scene m7_oh_storm_wood
when topic=weather here.region=oakhaven here.weather=storm
a : Trees groaning. Hear them? | A big limb came down on the Southway. | Keep out from under the old oaks today. | Something cracked out there. Big.
b[job=woodcutter] #wood : There'll be oak across every track by morning. Good wood, though.
b : They're talking. | Road's shut, then. | Old oaks drop limbs out of spite. | Big tree, big crack.
a@wood : Always good wood, to you.
a : Arguing. | Again. | Spite trees. | Poor thing.

scene m7_oh_summer_glade
when topic=weather|day here.region=oakhaven here.season=summer here.weather=clear|overcast
a : Cool under the trees, even now. | The glades are full of butterflies. | Bees in the hollow oak again. Hear them? | Green light through the leaves. Like being underwater.
b : Best place in summer. | Pups chasing them. | Good honey year. | Nicer than water.
a : And winter. | Never catch one. | Sweet. | Drier.

scene m7_oh_story_night
when topic=lore here.region=oakhaven here.time=dusk|night
a : The old ones say the Warmwood was one tree once, and the rest are its children. | Ever wonder why there's never beech on the Acorn Inn's fire? Bad luck to burn a beech. | My granddam told of a joiner who carved a door in an oak, and something knocked back. | There's a ring of toadstools by the sawmill. Nobody mows it.
b[stage=young] #pup : And then what? Then what?
b : Big family. | Why bad luck? | Did he open it? | Nobody's that brave.
a@pup : Then you go to bed, and I'll tell you the rest tomorrow.
a : And they all argue. | The beech remembers. | His widow did. Once. | Or that daft.

scene m7_oh_lore_green_wolf
when topic=lore here.region=oakhaven
a : They say there's a wolf in the Warmwood made of moss and leaves. | Leave the first honey on a stump. For the wood. | If you get lost, turn your coat inside out. The wood lets you go. | The boundary oak drinks the rain first. Before anyone.
b : My cousin met him. Came back quiet. | Stumps get fat, here. | Then everyone sees your lining. | Thirsty oak.
a : Quiet's the wood. | Fat stumps, lucky town. | Better lining than lost. | Old and greedy.

scene m7_oh_fest
when topic=festival here.region=oakhaven
a : They're stringing lanterns between the oaks for #here.festival#. | There's to be a dance in the big glade. | The joiners have made a new table for #here.festival#. Forty feet of oak. | Somebody always brings turnips.
b : The fireflies'll be jealous. | Mind the roots, dancing. | And not a nail in it, I'll bet. | Somebody always eats them.
a : Ha. | I'll mind your feet. | Not one. Pegs. | It's always you.

# ================================================================ Mirebridge: the Sallow Fens

scene m7_mb_fog_dusk
when topic=weather here.region=mirebridge here.weather=fog here.time=dusk|night
a : Mist bell's going. | Fog's come up off the lode quick as a thief. | Lanterns on the causeway look like the other kind. | Can't tell the river from the road.
b[from=mirebridge] #fen : Stay on the stones. Count them home. | Fen mist. Smell it? Peat and old secrets. | Mind the soft side. It's hungry this time of year.
b[from=ridgemere|frosthollow|coldwater|larkfield|southmeadow|oakhaven] #stranger : How does anyone find their way in this? | I can't see my paws. Are they still on? | Is that a light out there? Someone's waving.
b : Somebody's out. | It always is. | Count them. If there's one too many, walk faster. | Then stand still.
a@fen : Forty-one to the inn. | Mostly peat. | Always hungry, that side.
a@stranger : Slowly. And dry-footed. | Still on. Don't look down. | Don't look at it. Don't wave back.
a : Pray it's no pup. | Thief fog. | There's one too many. | I am.

scene m7_mb_flood
when topic=weather|day here.region=mirebridge here.weather=rain|storm
a : River's up a stride since yesterday. | Water's at the stilts. | Peat stacks are floating. | The lodes are brimming.
b[job=farmer|herder] #farm : I've moved the beasts to the knoll.
b : It'll top the causeway by morning. | Good thing they're stilts. | Fish the peat. | Fen's thirsty, it'll drink.
a@farm : Bring them to ours if it rises.
a : We'll be boats again. | Grandad's stilts. | Wet peat. | Fen's never thirsty.

scene m7_mb_summer_stink
when topic=weather here.region=mirebridge here.season=summer
a : The fen smells this time of year. | Midges in clouds over the lode. | Frogs all night. All night. | Bog's steaming in the heat.
b[from=mirebridge] #fen : That's the smell of home. Breathe it.
b : Stinks. | Smoke them out. | Lullaby. | Breathing, they say.
a@fen : I'm breathing through my mouth.
a : Sweet as a cellar. | With what? | Loud lullaby. | Bad breath.

scene m7_mb_winter_ice
when topic=weather here.region=mirebridge here.season=winter here.weather=clear|overcast|snow
a : Lodes are iced over. You can skate to the river. | Frost on the reeds. Pretty. | The fen's quiet in winter. Even the frogs sleep. | Ice cracks like a whip at night.
b[stage=young] #pup : Can we skate? Can we?
b : Mind the soft spots. | Brittle, too. | Don't wake them. | Ice talking.
a@pup : Near the bank. Only near the bank.
a : Mind everything. | Like glass. | Never. | Saying what?

scene m7_mb_eel_feast
when topic=festival here.region=mirebridge
a : Smoked eel for #here.festival#, eel pie, eel stew. | They'll light the causeway with rushlights. | Everyone lights a twist from the Eel and Lantern's hearth. | Reed boats for the pups to race.
b[from=oakhaven|larkfield|southmeadow|ridgemere|frosthollow|coldwater] #stranger : Is there anything that isn't eel?
b : Eel jelly too. | Prettiest road in the realm. | Old fire, new year. | Mine always sinks.
a@stranger : Bread. Under the eel.
a : Lovely. | Prettiest. | Every year. | Mine too.

scene m7_mb_lore_delta
when topic=lore here.region=mirebridge
a : They say the Drowned Delta was a kingdom once. | My uncle found a bronze ring in the peat. Old as anything. | Nobody digs where the reeds grow red. | Peat-cutters found a boat in the bog once. Miles from any water.
b[honesty=frank|guarded] #frank : The fen's given me nothing but wet boots.
b : Kingdoms drown. | Sell it. | Why red? | Water moves. Fens remember.
a@frank : Good boots?
a : Even here. | He wears it. | Nobody says. | Fens remember everything.

# ================================================================ Larkfield: the downs

scene m7_lf_wind_downs
when topic=weather here.region=larkfield here.weather=clear|overcast
a : Wind's coming over the High Downs. | Cloud shadows racing across the hills. | Larks up already, singing. | You can see clear to the Orchard Vale from the top today.
b[job=herder] #shep : Sheep hate a wind. They all face the same way and sulk.
b : Never stops up here. | Like sheep running. | Mad little birds. | And a lot of sheep.
a@shep : Like the council.
a : Never. | Like sheep. | Happy, though. | Mostly sheep.

scene m7_lf_rain_road
when topic=weather|day here.region=larkfield here.weather=rain|storm
a : The Downs Road's a river of chalk. | Carters stuck at the ford. | Every wagon's coming in white to the axle. | Chalk mud. Worst of all muds.
b[job=carter] #carter : I've unloaded twice and reloaded twice. Don't ask.
b : Pale and slippery. | Nobody's moving today. | Like flour paste. | Sticks like glue.
a@carter : I won't, then.
a : Like custard. | Everything's stuck. | Bread mud. | Worst.

scene m7_lf_market_carters
when topic=day here.region=larkfield here.day=market
a : Carters in from every road today. | Heard the news from Ridgemere before it's cold. | Somebody's selling sheep at a price that makes me laugh. | The inn's full of road dust and stories.
b[role=merchant] #trade : News is free. Sheep aren't.
b : Busy, busy. | Fresh from the city, is it? | Laugh and walk on. | Half true, the stories.
a@trade : Everything's for sale in Larkfield.
a : Good for trade. | Stale by the time it's here. | I did. | Half's plenty.

scene m7_lf_shearing
when topic=day|weather here.region=larkfield here.season=spring|summer
a : Shearing starts Hearthday. | Fleeces piled to the rafters. | Sheep look naked and cross. | My arms ache from the shears.
b[job=herder|farmer] #shep : Shear in the cold and they shiver. Shear in the heat and you do.
b : Good year for wool? | Mossgrave'll want them all. | Naked and cross, like me in the morning. | Rest them.
a@shep : I'll shear in the middle.
a : Heavy fleeces. | Mossgrave wants everything. | Ha. | Can't.

scene m7_lf_lore_chalk
when topic=lore here.region=larkfield
a : Who cut the white horse on the down? | Dig in the chalk, you find shells. Up here. On a hill. | They say there's a hollow hill where the old kings sleep. | There's a standing stone on the down that turns round in the dead of night, they say.
b[job=farmer|herder] #farm : I ploughed too close to the old stones once. The share broke. I'd not try again.
b : Older than Larkfield. | Sea was up here once, they say. | They can sleep. | Who's watched it?
a@farm : They don't like to be disturbed.
a : Older than talk. | Sea on a hill. | Let them. | Nobody that'll say.

scene m7_lf_fest_fair
when topic=festival here.region=larkfield
a : There'll be a sheep fair for #here.festival#. | Carters'll race wagons on the Downs Road. | Best fleece wins a ribbon. Mine'll lose. | Every inn bed taken for #here.festival#.
b : And a fight. Every year. | Wheels come off every year. | Brush it. | Sleep in the hay.
a : Good fight, though. | That's the fun. | Brushed it raw. | With the carters?

# ================================================================ Southmeadow: the Sunfields and the Orchard Vale

scene m7_sm_dust_storm
when topic=weather here.region=southmeadow here.weather=sandstorm
a : Topsoil's blowing off the Sunfields. | Dust on every sill. In the bread, even. | Can't see the orchard. | Shut the hives. They'll go mad.
b[job=farmer] #farm : That's next year's crop blowing away.
b : Where to? | Gritty loaf. | It's there. Choking. | Already did.
a@farm : Blow it back, then.
a : Larkfield, probably. | Crunchy. | Poor trees. | Good.

scene m7_sm_summer_heat
when topic=weather here.region=southmeadow here.season=summer here.weather=clear
a : Hot as a bread oven. | Shade under the apple trees, quick. | Grass is crackling. | Cider cellar's the only place to be.
b[from=frosthollow|coldwater] #north : I'm going to die. I'm a mountain wolf. I'm going to die here. | At home I'd be sat on a snowbank right now. | I've sat in the well bucket twice today.
b : Hotter. | Bagsy the big tree. | Fire weather. | Cellar's full.
a@north : Not today. Here, have some cider. | Here there's cider in the cellar. Better. | Third time's free.
a : And no bread. | Share it. | Watch the stubble. | Full of what?

scene m7_sm_orchard_blossom
when topic=weather|day here.region=southmeadow here.season=spring
a : The whole Orchard Vale's in blossom. | Petals like snow on the road. | Bees in every tree. | Frost tonight'd ruin it all.
b[job=farmer] #farm : I'll be up with fires in the rows if it frosts.
b : Smells like honey. | Prettier than snow. | Good. Bees mean apples. | Don't say it.
a@farm : I'll bring blankets.
a : Lovely. | Warmer, too. | Lots of apples. | Sorry.

scene m7_sm_harvest_dusk
when topic=day|weather here.region=southmeadow here.season=autumn here.time=dusk
a : Last cart's in. | Every barn's full. | Stubble fires down the valley. | Cider and bread tonight. Everyone.
b[job=farmer|laborer] #farm : My back's in the field somewhere. Somebody find it. | Thirty days, sunup to sundown. I could sleep a month. | Not a stook lost to rain this year. Not one.
b : Good year. | Thank the Bright One. | Pretty, burning. | Every soul.
a@farm : Cider finds backs. | Sleep, then. You've earned it. | Thank the Bright One.
a : Best in years. | And the rain. | Mind the hedges. | Every pup too.

scene m7_sm_rain_blessing
when topic=weather here.region=southmeadow here.weather=rain here.season=summer|spring
a : Rain! At last! | Listen to the fields drink. | Somebody's dancing in it. | Smell the dust settle.
b[stage=old] #old : Rain in the south's a gift. Never curse it here.
b : The well'll fill. | Glug glug. | Everybody, nearly. | Like a stone floor fresh washed.
a@old : I'll not. I'll dance.
a : Thank the gods. | Ha. | Me too. | Lovely, after all that dust.

scene m7_sm_lore_scarecrows
when topic=lore here.region=southmeadow
a : They say the scarecrows walk on harvest night. | Never wear a scarecrow's hat. Never. | My grandfather talked to his scarecrow. Said it answered. | Every field's scarecrow is named for a dead farmer.
b : Walk where? | Why? What happens? | Grandfathers do. | Remembering.
a : To the inn. | Nobody's lived to say. | It told him the weather. Wrong. | Watching the fields still.

scene m7_sm_fest_cider
when topic=festival here.region=southmeadow
a : First barrel of cider breached for #here.festival#. | The Apple Tree's full to the door. | Garlands on the Cider House. | They'll crown the best orchard.
b[from=frosthollow|coldwater|ridgemere] #north : Is all your cider this strong?
b : Smell it from here. | Spilling into the road. | Smells like heaven. | The Harrows. Every year.
a@north : Stronger. Sit down.
a : Taste it from here. | Join it. | Heaven with apples. | Every year.

# ================================================================ The wild: on the road between

scene m7_wild_road_weather
when topic=weather here.region=wild
a : Long way between roofs out here. | Weather's always worse on the road. | Sky looks big out here. | No shelter for miles.
b[job=carter] #carter : I know every hedge between here and the next town that keeps the rain off.
b : Keep walking. | It's the same. We just notice. | Big and empty. | Then don't stop.
a@carter : Show me one.
a : Legs are tired. | We notice. | Unnerving. | Right.

scene m7_wild_night_road
when topic=weather|lore here.region=wild here.time=dusk|night
a : Should've stopped at the last inn. | Did you hear that? In the hedge? | Not a light anywhere. Not one. | Keep the lantern up.
b : We'll make the next one. | Fox. Or worse. Fox. | Lamps are for towns. | It's up.
a : We'd better. | Say fox. | I'd give a lot for a town. | Higher.
